{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>YHWH came from Sinai</b>. He says that HaShem came from Sinai to Israel, for from there He caused His Shekhinah to dwell among them, and it never again departed from them. The matter is that the Glory descended on Mount Sinai, as it is written (Exodus 19:20), \"And YHWH came down upon Mount Sinai, to the top of the mountain, and YHWH called Moses,\" etc., and there it was all the days that Moses was going up and coming down, as it is written (Exodus 24:17), \"And the appearance of the glory of YHWH was like devouring fire on the top of the mountain,\" etc. And when the latter tablets were given to him, the Glory dwelled in the Tent of Meeting, as it is written (Exodus 33:9), \"And it came to pass, when Moses entered the tent, the pillar of cloud descended and stood at the door of the tent, and spoke with Moses,\" and it is written (Exodus 33:11), \"And YHWH spoke to Moses face to face,\" etc. And when the Tabernacle was erected, the Glory dwelled in the Tabernacle, as it is written (Exodus 40:34), \"And the cloud covered the tent of meeting, and the glory of YHWH filled the Tabernacle.\" And from there all the divine communications came to him all the days of the wilderness." "<b>And He shone forth from Seir to them</b>. For after they journeyed from Sinai on their first march, the cloud dwelled in the wilderness of Paran, and from there he sent spies, as it is written (Exodus 13:3) — rather (Numbers 13:3), \"And Moses sent them from the wilderness of Paran.\" And the people stumbled, and there was no divine communication with Moses until they came to Seir, at the border of the children of Esau, at the end of the forty years (Deuteronomy 2:2), as it is written there (Deuteronomy 33:7) — rather (Deuteronomy 2:7), \"For YHWH your Elohim has blessed you in all the work of your hand; He has known your walking through this great wilderness these forty years; YHWH your Elohim has been with you.\" And behold, then, when they came to Seir, HaShem was to them for an everlasting light and the days of their mourning were completed, and He commanded them to beware of Seir and Ammon and Moab, and said that they would begin to possess their land, and they inherited the land of Sihon and Og, the two great kings of the Amorite." "<b>He appeared from Mount Paran</b>. That He looked upon them and set their affairs as the light of His countenance from Mount Paran. And the reason is that the beginning of their entering the great wilderness was from Paran, as it is written (Numbers 10:12), \"And they journeyed,\" etc., \"from the wilderness of Sinai, and the cloud rested in the wilderness of Paran.\" And behold, HaShem appeared upon them to see what they needed in the great and awesome wilderness, from the language of (Job 10:3), \"and upon the counsel of the wicked You appear\" — You looked and saw in Your great light all the hidden places of their thoughts." "<b>And He came from among ten thousand holy ones, from His right hand a fiery law to them</b>. He says that the Glory came to Israel from among ten thousand holy ones of the Most High, who descended with Him on the mountain, as in the matter that is written (Psalms 68:18), \"The chariots of Elohim are twenty thousand, even thousands upon thousands; HaShem is among them, Sinai in holiness.\" <b>From His right hand a fiery law to them</b> — the meaning is: from the right hand of His splendor was a fiery law to them, not from the hand of the mentioned angels, but from the right hand of HaShem Himself in His glory. And it is possible that <b>מֵרִבְבֹת קֹדֶשׁ</b> means \"from the abundance of His holiness,\" for Holy, Holy, Holy is YHWH of Hosts in every manner of holiness." "<b>And the meaning of a fiery law to them</b> is like <b>a law of fire</b>, as He said (Deuteronomy 5:21), \"And we heard His voice from the midst of the fire.\" Or its meaning is like <b>fire and a law to them</b> — He mentions that He showed them the supernal fire and made them hear the law, and this is as in the matter that is written (Deuteronomy 4:36), \"And upon the earth He showed you His great fire, and you heard His words from the midst of the fire.\" And by way of truth, he also indicates that the fire, which is the law, is from His right hand, for the attribute of judgment is included within mercy. And according to the opinion of our Sages, the verse means that He came from Sinai to give Torah to Israel; that He shone forth from Seir to them, to Israel — for He revealed Himself to the children of Esau to give them the Torah, and when the children of Esau did not wish to receive it, He illuminated His face toward Israel and gave it to them with His right hand and His arm, and with the light of His countenance, for He desired them. And He appeared and showed them the splendor and majesty of His countenance from Mount Paran, when the children of Ishmael did not want the Torah. He mentions that it was great merit for Israel, that none of the other nations wished to receive the Torah. And behold, he mentioned all the seed of Abraham, that not one of them desired the Torah except Israel; but upon all the nations too He revealed Himself to give it to them, and they did not want it, according to what is found in the tradition of our Sages (Avodah Zarah 2b)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :register {:fixes 1}, :notes {1 "Ramban cites verse addresses that do not always match the references; where his citation appears to point to a different verse than the one quoted, I have translated his text literally and added 'rather' with the correct address."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:16:08.268183344Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}